# How a card reaches a house, drawn the board way.
#
# The worked example for `docs/atlas/45-board.md`: ONE diagram, with the detail hanging on
# the nodes it is about rather than standing as pictures of its own.
#
# Push it as the talker's own board:
#
#     keel board push --talker --file tests/fixtures/example.board
#
# `tests/test_boards.py` (ExampleBoardTest) parses it, so what the docs show is what draws.

board "How a card reaches a house"

group board "On the board"
group house "In the house"

node inbox file "inbox" in board "where a card is filed"
node assigned file "assigned" in board "the user said start"
node drain bot "the drain" in board "one pass every poll"
node contract file "house contract" in house "read first"
node worktree folder "the worktree" in house "the card's own checkout"
node review file "review" in house "the outcome gate"

edge inbox -> assigned "the user's tap"
edge assigned -> drain "picked up on the next pass"
edge drain -> contract "starts a house"
edge contract -> worktree "builds there"
edge worktree -> review "stages its outcome"

mark assigned accent
mark review warn

note board "Two places, and the drain is the only door between them: nothing on the board starts a house by itself, and nothing in a house approves its own gate."
note review "The outcome gate is the user's tap, never a command the house can run."

step inbox "A card is filed in the inbox"
step assigned "The user assigns it"
step drain "The drain starts its house on the next pass"
step worktree "The house builds in its own worktree"
step review "It stages the outcome for the user's Approve"

table board "Who moves a card"
cols Stage | Moved by | How
row inbox | the user or the talker | kb create
row assigned | the user | a tap on the deck
row review | the house | kb stage
row done | the user | Approve outcome
